\subsection{Simulated Longitudinal Impact Analysis}
To evaluate potential behavioral impact, we developed a **synthetic agent-based simulation** modeling campus schedules and student movement patterns. The simulation emulates a cohort of 50 virtual students following scripted compliance/non-compliance behaviors over an 8-week period. Agent transition probabilities are fixed and not learned.

\textbf{Simulation Design:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Baseline Phase (Weeks 1-3):} Agents operate under "silent monitoring" with no alert feedback. Non-compliance events are logged but do not influence agent behavior.
    \item \textbf{Intervention Phase (Weeks 4-8):} Alert mechanisms are activated. Agents probabilistically adjust behavior based on simulated alert reception ($P_{compliance} = 0.7$ post-alert).
\end{itemize}

**Results:** The simulation shows a **40\% reduction in scripted deviation events** when comparing baseline (Weeks 1-3) against intervention phases (Weeks 4-8). This demonstrates internal logical consistency of the alert–response dynamics within a synthetic agent-based model, without claiming real-world behavioral efficacy. This reduction reflects internal logical response consistency within a synthetic agent model and should not be interpreted as empirical student behavior change.

\textbf{Limitations:} These results reflect **synthetic agent behavior in a controlled model**, not empirical student outcomes. Agent decision models use simplified probabilistic rules and may not capture the complexity of real student motivations, social dynamics, or institutional culture.

