% ==========================================
% IX. DISCUSSION
% ==========================================
\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Ethical and Institutional Considerations}
The deployment of spatiotemporal evaluation requires strict adherence to privacy norms to ensure it is used as a tool for safety and schedule adherence rather than surveillance \cite{b19}.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Opt-In Policy:} Tracking is limited to academic zones and hours. Dormitories and private areas are strictly blacklisted from the logic engine.
    \item \textbf{Role-Based Access Control (RBAC):} Only authorized academic deans and counselors possess the cryptographic keys required to decrypt student identity logs.
    \item \textbf{Non-Punitive Design:} System alerts are designed as informational prompts for counseling, not automated mechanisms for disciplinary action.
\end{itemize}

Subject tokens are session-scoped, non-reversible, and regenerated periodically; the system does not support longitudinal identity tracking. The system is not designed for continuous behavioral surveillance. Evaluation is restricted to predefined academic zones during scheduled academic hours, and no free-movement profiling, trajectory reconstruction, or long-term mobility analytics are performed.

\subsection{Operational Challenges}
While the experimental results are promising, real-world deployment faces distinct challenges. The most significant is **Wireless Signal Shadowing**. In dense concrete buildings, WiFi RSSI can fluctuate, causing temporary network partitions. Our Merkle Tree sync implementation mitigates data loss, but real-time alerting is delayed during these outages. A potential solution involves deploying a secondary LoRaWAN mesh network for low-bandwidth, high-reliability alert transmission.

\subsection{Security Architecture}
Deploying a monitoring system introduces potential cybersecurity risks. We structured our defense in layers to prevent both external attacks and internal tampering.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Database Injection Attacks:} An attacker might attempt SQL Injection to alter their schedule (e.g., marking themselves as "Free" during a Math exam). We use Parameterized Queries (Prepared Statements) for all database interactions. The input is treated as a literal value, never as executable code, neutralizing this threat completely.
    \item \textbf{Sensor Spoofing:} A student could hold up a high-resolution photo of a compliant student to "trick" the camera. While this paper focuses on logic, the underlying vision layer incorporates Non-visual liveness signal (external), which would flag a static photo as an invalid subject \cite{b10}. Privacy-preserving obfuscation methods such as those proposed by Brunton can further mitigate surveillance risks \cite{b20}.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion and Future Scope}
This work demonstrates that reliable academic analytics can be achieved through secure, schedule-aware compliance mechanisms operating on pre-existing identity labels. By shifting the approach from indiscriminate surveillance to targeted, logic-based compliance monitoring, we achieved high detection accuracy for schedule deviations while strictly adhering to data minimization principles.

Future work will explore:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Predictive Analytics:} Utilizing Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) to analyze historical attendance patterns and predict students at high risk of dropping out.
    \item \textbf{Blockchain Logging:} Implementing an immutable ledger for alert logs to prevent tampering by internal actors.
    \item \textbf{Smart Building Integration:} Linking the detection system to access control mechanisms, informing academic advisors of recurring schedule deviations for early, non-punitive intervention.
\end{itemize}

\section*{Salami-Slicing and Redundancy Declaration}
This paper addresses spatiotemporal schedule compliance logic within the ScholarMaster Research Series. It does not reuse datasets, evaluation metrics, or analytical results from companion papers on engagement inference, privacy-preserving sensing, or biometric scalability. Each paper targets a distinct architectural layer with transparent cross-referencing to ensure ethical modularity.

\section*{Acknowledgment}
We acknowledge the administrative staff who assisted in defining the schedule constraints and the volunteers who participated in the simulations.

